# NetBird 0.77.1 (combined server) + dashboard 2.91.1 environment schema
# (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# Source: grep of os.Getenv / os.LookupEnv across combined/, management/,
# signal/, relay/, idp/ and shared/ at tag v0.77.1, and of docker/
# init_react_envs.sh + config.json at dashboard tag v2.91.1.
#
# The JSON-vs-env split, because the docs describe a different deployment:
# this template runs the ALL-IN-ONE `netbirdio/netbird-server` image, whose
# only configuration surface is /etc/netbird/config.yaml. combined/cmd/
# config.go is plain yaml.v3 with no env binding, and the image's CMD is
# `--config /etc/netbird/config.yaml`, its single flag. The classic
# management.json + setup.env NETBIRD_* family on
# docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/environment-variables belongs to the SPLIT
# netbirdio/management + signal + relay + coturn stack and to configure.sh,
# none of which run here; and the "any --flag becomes NB_FLAG_NAME" mapping
# in that page exists only in relay/cmd/env.go and signal/cmd/env.go, i.e.
# in the standalone relay/signal binaries. So the server half of this file
# is exactly the explicit os.Getenv knobs compiled into the management,
# signal and relay libraries, nothing more. The compose sets NO environment
# on the netbird service at all: everything that install actually needs is
# in the rendered config.yaml.
#
# NB_PPROF_ADDR is read (combined/cmd/pprof.go) but sits behind a `pprof`
# build tag that release builds do not set, so it is inert in the published
# image and is not declared below.
#
# Auth, plainly: this template is SELF-CONTAINED. config.yaml sets
# server.auth.issuer and localAuthDisabled: false, which starts the embedded
# Dex IdP inside the same container, so no external IdP is required to get
# the first admin in. That first admin is created by an UNAUTHENTICATED
# POST /api/setup (management/server/http/handlers/instance/
# instance_handler.go: "These endpoints bypass authentication for initial
# setup"), which only works while no owner exists. Whoever reaches the
# dashboard first after deploy owns the network. Finish setup immediately.
# An external IdP is optional and is configured in config.yaml
# (server.auth connectors), never through the environment.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("NetBird self-hosted configuration files", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/maintenance/configuration-files)
# ---

# ─── Stack identity and secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Public base URL of this install, scheme included, for example
# vpn.example.com prefixed with https. Not a bare hostname: it is
# interpolated into
# config.yaml as server.exposedAddress and as the OIDC issuer
# `${NETBIRD_DOMAIN}/oauth2`, and the dashboard runs it through a URL
# validator (src/utils/config.ts) that falls back to a localhost URL when
# it does not parse. Peers and the registered auth redirect URIs are pinned to
# it, so changing it later re-enrols every peer.
# @required @type=url
# @docs("Self-hosting quickstart", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/selfhosted-quickstart)
NETBIRD_DOMAIN=

# Shared secret the local relay authenticates peers with (config.yaml
# server.authSecret; the relay's HMAC credential). Whoever holds it can mint
# relay credentials and join traffic to the relay, so treat it as a network
# credential, not a config value. Rendered into config.yaml at deploy, never
# read from the process environment.
# @required @sensitive
NETBIRD_AUTH_SECRET=

# Encrypts sensitive columns of the management datastore at rest
# (config.yaml store.encryptionKey; util/crypt.NewFieldEncrypt
# base64-decodes it and demands exactly 32 bytes). Blast radius runs both
# ways: leak it and the peer records are readable, lose it and the existing
# store cannot be decrypted at all. Unique per install.
# @required @sensitive
NETBIRD_STORE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ─── Dashboard: OIDC wiring (set by the compose) ──────────────────────────

# Management API origin the dashboard calls; the compose points it at this
# stack's own public URL. The entrypoint strips a trailing :80/:443 and
# derives the CSP connect-src (and the ws:// vs wss:// choice) from it.
NETBIRD_MGMT_API_ENDPOINT=${NETBIRD_DOMAIN}
# Same origin for the gRPC-web/wasm client the dashboard embeds.
NETBIRD_MGMT_GRPC_API_ENDPOINT=${NETBIRD_DOMAIN}

# OIDC client id and audience. Neither is free-form here: the combined
# server hard-codes the audience it accepts
# (combined/cmd/config.go: httpConfig.AuthAudience = "netbird-dashboard")
# and the embedded Dex registers exactly one dashboard client with that id
# (management/server/idp/embedded.go). Any other value fails at the
# authorize endpoint, or later on the API with an audience mismatch.
AUTH_AUDIENCE=netbird-dashboard
AUTH_CLIENT_ID=netbird-dashboard

# OIDC issuer. The /oauth2 suffix is load-bearing on both sides: the
# embedded IdP derives the dashboard base URL with
# strings.TrimSuffix(issuer, "/oauth2"), and this must equal config.yaml's
# server.auth.issuer character for character.
AUTH_AUTHORITY=${NETBIRD_DOMAIN}/oauth2

# Switches the dashboard off the Auth0-specific code path. Must stay false
# for the embedded IdP; true makes it read the AUTH0_* variables instead.
# @type=boolean
USE_AUTH0=false

# Scopes requested at /authorize. Dex validates them and rejects anything it
# does not recognise with invalid_scope (server/oauth2.go), so this is
# limited to openid, profile, email, groups, offline_access and federated:id.
AUTH_SUPPORTED_SCOPES=openid profile email groups

# Redirect paths, appended to window.location.origin by the dashboard. Dex
# matches redirect_uri literally against the list config.yaml registers
# (dashboardRedirectURIs), and the dashboard's built-in default is
# "/#callback", which that list does not contain, so both are set explicitly
# to the registered pair.
AUTH_REDIRECT_URI=/nb-auth
AUTH_SILENT_REDIRECT_URI=/nb-silent-auth

# Which token the dashboard sends to the management API. Default is
# accessToken; with the embedded Dex both are signed JWTs carrying
# aud=netbird-dashboard, and the compose pins idToken.
# @type=enum(accessToken, idToken)
NETBIRD_TOKEN_SOURCE=idToken

# ─── Dashboard: optional auth overrides ───────────────────────────────────

# Only for a confidential OIDC client at an EXTERNAL IdP. The embedded Dex
# registers the dashboard as a public PKCE client, so leave this empty;
# setting it ships a client secret into browser-readable config.json.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Self-hosted identity providers", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/identity-providers)
AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Legacy Auth0 fallbacks, read only when the matching AUTH_* variable above
# is unset (init_react_envs.sh). Irrelevant to this template; listed because
# leaving one set while clearing an AUTH_* twin silently changes the issuer.
AUTH0_DOMAIN=
AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH0_AUDIENCE=

# NetBird-cloud auth-service origin, added to the CSP when set. Empty for a
# self-hosted install.
NETBIRD_AUTH_SERVICE_URL=

# Extra origins appended to the dashboard's Content-Security-Policy
# (connect-src, frame-src, script-src). Only needed when an external IdP or
# asset host would otherwise be blocked; every entry widens the policy.
NETBIRD_CSP=

# ─── Dashboard: TLS inside the container ──────────────────────────────────

# Runs certbot --nginx inside the dashboard container on boot and installs a
# renewal cron. otterdeploy's edge already terminates TLS in front of the
# published port 80, so leaving these empty is correct; setting them makes
# the container fight the edge for the certificate and for :443.
# @docs("External reverse proxy", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/external-reverse-proxy)
LETSENCRYPT_DOMAIN=
# @type=email
LETSENCRYPT_EMAIL=
# HTTPS port passed to certbot, only read by that script.
# @type=port
NGINX_SSL_PORT=

# ─── Dashboard: feature flags and analytics ───────────────────────────────

# Carries the query string of the dashboard URL through to the auth layer.
# @type=boolean
NETBIRD_DRAG_QUERY_PARAMS=false

# Path the dashboard loads its embedded wasm NetBird client from. Empty uses
# the version pinned in the image.
NETBIRD_WASM_PATH=

# Enterprise-license and cloud-tenancy flags. Both belong to NetBird's own
# builds; on a self-hosted community install they stay false.
# @type=boolean
NETBIRD_LICENSED=false
# @type=boolean
NETBIRD_CLOUD=false

# Agent Network (LLM gateway) surfaces. ENABLED adds them to the normal
# dashboard; ONLY hides every standard NetBird page and leaves just those,
# which is the preset upstream pairs with a private proxy.
# @type=boolean
NETBIRD_AGENT_NETWORK_ENABLED=false
# @type=boolean
NETBIRD_AGENT_NETWORK_ONLY=false

# Third-party analytics injected into the dashboard's HTML and CSP. All
# empty by default, which keeps a self-hosted dashboard from calling out.
NETBIRD_GOOGLE_ANALYTICS_ID=
NETBIRD_GOOGLE_TAG_MANAGER_ID=
NETBIRD_HOTJAR_TRACK_ID=
NETBIRD_HUBSPOT_PORTAL_ID=
NETBIRD_HUBSPOT_SIGNUP_FORM_ID=
NETBIRD_HUBSPOT_ONBOARDING_FORM_ID=
NETBIRD_HUBSPOT_SURVEY_FORM_ID=
# Comma-separated emails excluded from the analytics above.
NETBIRD_ANALYTICS_EXCLUDED_EMAILS=

# ─── Management server: authentication and abuse control ──────────────────

# "true" lets the unauthenticated POST /api/setup that creates the first
# owner ALSO return a Personal Access Token in its response body
# (management/server/instance/setup_service.go), valid one day by default.
# Convenient for wiring otterdeploy's private-networking integration in one
# step, and a full-API credential handed to whoever calls that endpoint
# first. Prefer creating the PAT from the dashboard, or from
# `netbird-server admin token create` inside the container.
# @docs("Self-hosted admin CLI", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/maintenance/admin-cli)
NB_SETUP_PAT_ENABLED=

# AES key encrypting the embedded IdP's session cookies; also settable as
# config.yaml server.auth.sessionCookieEncryptionKey, which wins when both
# are present (idp/embedded.go resolveSessionCookieEncryptionKey). Must be
# 16, 24 or 32 raw bytes, or base64 that decodes to one of those. Empty
# leaves the cookies unencrypted; a leak of this key is a leak of live
# admin sessions.
# @sensitive
NB_IDP_SESSION_COOKIE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Per-client rate limiting on the management HTTP API. It is OFF unless this
# is exactly "true" (management/server/http/middleware/rate_limiter.go).
# Worth turning on for an install whose API is reachable from the internet,
# which this one is.
# @type=boolean
NB_API_RATE_LIMITING_ENABLED=
# Requests per minute once enabled; a non-numeric or non-positive value
# falls back to 6.
# @type=number
NB_API_RATE_LIMITING_RPM=6
# Burst allowance once enabled; same fallback rule, default 500.
# @type=number
NB_API_RATE_LIMITING_BURST=500

# "true" refuses a login when another peer already holds the same WireGuard
# key, instead of taking it over (management/internals/shared/grpc/
# server.go). LOG_BLOCKED_PEERS only logs those attempts.
# @type=boolean
NB_BLOCK_SAME_PEERS=
# @type=boolean
NB_LOG_BLOCKED_PEERS=

# Cap on concurrent peer sync streams. A negative value disables the limit.
# @type=number
NB_MAX_CONCURRENT_SYNCS=1000

# ─── Management server: datastore ─────────────────────────────────────────

# Store engine override. It is read ONLY when the config file leaves the
# engine empty (management/server/store/store.go getStoreEngine), and this
# template's config.yaml sets store.engine: sqlite, so this variable does
# nothing here. Upstream's own comment calls it test-oriented. Switch
# engines in config.yaml instead.
# @type=enum(sqlite, postgres, mysql)
# @docs("Management Postgres store", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/maintenance/postgres-store)
NETBIRD_STORE_ENGINE=

# DSNs used when the engine actually is postgres or mysql. config.yaml's
# store.dsn is the supported place to put these; the NB_ pair below is the
# fallback the store layer reads, and NETBIRD_-prefixed twins are the legacy
# names checked second (store.go lookupDSNEnv).
# @sensitive
NB_STORE_ENGINE_POSTGRES_DSN=
# @sensitive
NB_STORE_ENGINE_MYSQL_DSN=
# @sensitive
NETBIRD_STORE_ENGINE_POSTGRES_DSN=
# @sensitive
NETBIRD_STORE_ENGINE_MYSQL_DSN=

# Absolute path of the SQLite file. combined/cmd/root.go exports this itself
# from config.yaml store.file when that is set, so it only applies when the
# config leaves it empty; the default is store.db under dataDir, which the
# named volume covers.
NB_STORE_ENGINE_SQLITE_FILE=

# Max open SQL connections. Defaults to the container's CPU count and is
# forced to 1 on SQLite, where setting it is logged as unsupported.
# @type=number
NB_SQL_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=

# Go duration bounding a single store transaction; default 5m.
NB_STORE_TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT=5m

# Redis URL for the shared account/IdP cache; without it the cache is
# in-process (gocache), which is what a single-container install wants. The
# URL usually carries a password, hence sensitive.
# @sensitive
NB_CACHE_REDIS_ADDRESS=
# Legacy name for the same setting, read only when the one above is empty.
# @sensitive
NB_IDP_CACHE_REDIS_ADDRESS=

# ─── Management server: activity events ───────────────────────────────────

# Account activity log. Note the polarity: the code treats an UNSET value as
# enabled and only "true" or an empty string keep it on
# (management/server/event.go isEnabled), so set it to "false" to turn the
# activity feed off.
# @type=boolean
NB_EVENT_ACTIVITY_LOG_ENABLED=true

# Where those events are stored. combined/cmd/root.go exports all three from
# config.yaml's activityStore block when present, so these are only read
# when that block is absent; the default is events.db under dataDir.
# @type=enum(sqlite, postgres)
# @docs("Activity events Postgres store", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/maintenance/activity-postgres-store)
NB_ACTIVITY_EVENT_STORE_ENGINE=
# @sensitive
NB_ACTIVITY_EVENT_POSTGRES_DSN=
NB_ACTIVITY_EVENT_SQLITE_FILE=

# ─── Management, signal and relay: tuning ─────────────────────────────────

# Geolocation lookups for posture checks. "true" skips initialising the
# GeoLite database entirely, which also avoids its periodic download.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Geolocation database", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/maintenance/geo-support)
NB_DISABLE_GEOLOCATION=

# How long a network-map update is buffered before it is pushed to peers,
# in milliseconds, and how long after boot the server waits before applying
# that value. Unset means 1ms of buffering, which is the safe default; both
# are raise-under-load knobs for large accounts.
# @type=number
# @docs("Scaling a self-hosted deployment", https://docs.netbird.io/selfhosted/maintenance/scaling/scaling-your-self-hosted-deployment)
NB_PEER_UPDATE_INTERVAL_MS=
# @type=number
NB_PEER_UPDATE_STARTUP_PERIOD_S=

# Go duration the account-request buffer coalesces store reads over;
# default 100ms.
NB_GET_ACCOUNT_BUFFER_INTERVAL=100ms

# Go duration for calls out to an external IdP; default 10s. Only matters
# when config.yaml points at one.
NB_IDP_TIMEOUT=10s

# Go duration slept before the first IdP cache warm-up. Unset skips the
# delay; an invalid duration is a hard startup error, not a warning.
NB_IDP_CACHE_WARMUP_DELAY=

# Go duration the embedded signal server waits when pushing a message to a
# peer; default 10s. Values that do not parse, or are not positive, are
# ignored.
NB_SIGNAL_SEND_TIMEOUT=10s

# Missed health-check rounds the embedded relay tolerates before it drops a
# peer connection; default 1.
# @type=number
NB_RELAY_HC_ATTEMPT_THRESHOLD=1

# Port range the reverse-proxy module auto-assigns L4 listeners from. An
# inverted range is rejected at init and both fall back to the defaults.
# @type=port
NB_PROXY_PORT_MIN=10000
# @type=port
NB_PROXY_PORT_MAX=49151

# Rows per batch when streaming a proxy snapshot; default 500, and any
# value that is not a positive integer is ignored.
# @type=number
NB_PROXY_SNAPSHOT_BATCH_SIZE=500

# Interval, in seconds, of the anonymous usage-metrics push; default 12h.
# It only runs at all while config.yaml leaves disableAnonymousMetrics
# false, which is where you turn the push off.
# @type=number
NETBIRD_METRICS_INTERVAL_IN_SECONDS=
